{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15061 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Chaxamycin B appears to interact with DNA polymerase iota through a multi-step pathway involving a sialyltransferase enzyme. The evidence suggests that Chaxamycin B targets CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which in turn is targeted by multiple compounds, including (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and 1-Hydroxycyclocolorenone. These compounds, in turn, are linked to DNA polymerase iota as their targets. This indicates that Chaxamycin B may exert its effect through a chain of molecular interactions, where the sialyltransferase acts as a bridge between the antibiotic and the DNA polymerase. The presence of multiple target compounds further supports the idea that Chaxamycin B may act through a shared mechanism, possibly by modulating the activity of the sialyltransferase, which is critical for the function of DNA polymerase iota. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the interaction network, emphasizing the role of the sialyltransferase in linking Chaxamycin B to DNA polymerase iota.",
